# Decision (see SESSION_LOG.md, "Infrastructure — GitHub backup"): data,
# output, and logs are now tracked in git rather than excluded. This was a
# deliberate reversal of Session 1.1's original "regenerated by pipeline,
# not source-controlled" stance -- the user wants this repo to double as a
# backup of real, not-easily-re-obtainable snapshot files (manual DK/FD
# salary exports, a specific day's nflverse parquet pull) in addition to
# code, since those aren't trivially regenerable if lost. All current data
# files are small (largest is well under 1MB), comfortably clear of
# GitHub's 50MB warning / 100MB hard-limit thresholds.
#
# Still worth revisiting if either of these changes:
#   1. A future session adds a genuinely large file (e.g. a full multi-
#      season parquet pull, not just one week/season) -- consider Git LFS
#      or moving just that file to external storage rather than reverting
#      this whole decision.
#   2. Any file starts containing something sensitive -- this repo has no
#      PII/credentials in data/ today (public NFL stats + a personal DK/FD
#      salary export, not sensitive), but re-check before tracking a new
#      data source blindly.

# Wrangler local cache (contains Cloudflare account ID/email) -- never commit
.wrangler/
data/_precip_cache.json

# ECR blend fetch cache (re-fetched every build; audit logs live in data/ecr_blend/)
data/ecr/

# Fantasy Cruncher subscription exports and anything derived from them (repo is PUBLIC -- never commit)
data/fc_history/
